PropInspect's deposit-law guides don't cover Washington, DC. They only cover the 50 states, so I can't give you a PropInspect guide link or review date for DC.

I searched the web instead. Most of what I found comes from landlord and tenant websites, not DC government pages, so double-check it against the official rules before you rely on it:

- **Deposit limit:** 
A deposit, or any other payment taken as security, can't be more than the first full month's rent. It can be charged only once per tenancy.

- **Interest and how the money is held:** The deposit 
must be held in an interest-bearing escrow account.
 
DC's disclosure form says that if a tenancy lasts more than 12 months, the deposit should be returned with the interest it earned.

- **Deadline to return it:** 
Within 45 days after the tenancy ends, the landlord must either return the deposit or send written notice that they intend to keep some of it. If they send that notice, they then have 30 days to send an itemized list and any remaining balance.

- **Penalty:** Several sites say a tenant can get up to three times the amount wrongly kept. One says 
treble damages require a finding of bad faith
, so it may not be automatic.
- **Where disputes go:** 
DC Code section 42-3502.17 lets the Office of Administrative Hearings handle complaints about deposits that weren't returned and interest that wasn't paid.

- **Official rules:** Sources point to DC Code § 42-3502.17 and 
14 DCMR §§ 308-311
, which are on the DC regulations website (dcregs.dc.gov).

This is general information, not legal advice. If you have a specific dispute, check the official DC code and regulations, or contact DC's Office of the Tenant Advocate or a local attorney.